 displayed connectives agree with classical truth functions on true and false, the source argues by induction for equality of the many-valued and classical evaluations. Its displayed valuation hypothesis restricts only one variable p, while the conclusion concerns an arbitrary formula A; the induction needs the valuation restricted to true and false on every variable occurring in A, or on all propositional variables. The source also leaves language and constant qualifications implicit. The accompanying caveat preserves all of these limitations without changing the proposition.","object_role":"proposition","record_sha256":"91d6f4d5ba9ab053a48cae078b40ef99facb04a9d7f893ce0de541acdc124e2d","review_decision":"Fresh source-reading shard; producer-side authoring, not independent acceptance.","source_formal_object":{"chapter_key":"mvl:syn","column":1,"content_end":1728771,"content_start":1727976,"environment_id":"projected-env-003338","file":"content/many-valued-logic/syntax-and-semantics/sublogics.tex","instance_id":"inst-00443","line":23,"name":"prop","object_class":"proposition","offset":853,"part_key":"mvl","scope":"projected","stream_end":1728781,"stream_start":1727964},"source_formal_object_sha256":"c27fd4d1ce6e369368eac951475f6d2f5fed34315910860024641a17c84055d7","source_order_index":18}
